1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,835
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,835 --> 00:00:08,515
This is chapter forty-three: integration testing and Testcontainers.

3
00:00:08,965 --> 00:00:10,786
Unit tests prove your logic.

4
00:00:10,786 --> 00:00:15,339
But some of the most expensive production bugs aren't in logic at all.

5
00:00:15,339 --> 00:00:20,933
They're in your assumptions about the database, the driver, and the services you call.

6
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:26,100
Here's a repository query: recent orders for a customer.

7
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:39,817
A unit test with a mocked DataSource stubs executeQuery to return a fake result set, and passes whether the SQL is valid, references a dropped column, or binds the timestamp wrong.

8
00:00:40,267 --> 00:00:43,615
Only a real database answers the real questions.

9
00:00:43,615 --> 00:00:46,684
Does the SQL run against the current schema?

10
00:00:46,684 --> 00:00:49,545
Does the unique constraint actually fire?

11
00:00:49,545 --> 00:00:54,009
Does an Instant survive the round trip without losing precision?

12
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:56,102
Does the driver accept a UUID?

13
00:00:56,102 --> 00:01:03,008
Each of those has shipped as a production incident, in codebases with excellent unit-test coverage.

14
00:01:03,908 --> 00:01:11,754
Unit tests prove logic, end-to-end tests prove the system, and integration tests prove your assumptions.

15
00:01:11,754 --> 00:01:18,620
The useful definition: an integration test crosses at least one boundary you don't control.

16
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:21,487
A database, a broker, an HTTP service.

17
00:01:21,937 --> 00:01:24,722
Testcontainers makes that practical.

18
00:01:24,722 --> 00:01:36,948
From the test JVM, it asks Docker for real Postgres, mapped to a random host port, and a sidecar called Ryuk removes it when the JVM exits, even after a kill.

19
00:01:36,948 --> 00:01:40,817
No shared test database, and no who broke staging.

20
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:42,979
Here's the shape.

21
00:01:42,979 --> 00:01:47,955
A static container, so it starts once per class, not once per test.

22
00:01:47,955 --> 00:01:55,605
Before all tests, build a DataSource from the container's JDBC URL, and run the real Flyway migrations.

23
00:01:55,605 --> 00:01:58,353
Truncate the tables before each test.

24
00:01:58,353 --> 00:02:01,621
Then insert two orders and check the cutoff.

25
00:02:02,071 --> 00:02:05,336
Running the real migrations isn't incidental.

26
00:02:05,336 --> 00:02:08,891
It makes the migration scripts a tested artifact.

27
00:02:08,891 --> 00:02:14,551
A migration that fails on an empty database fails this test, not a deployment.

28
00:02:15,451 --> 00:02:17,897
Test the constraint, not the code.

29
00:02:17,897 --> 00:02:27,825
Save twice with the same idempotency key, and assert the SQL state is twenty-three five oh five, the standard code for a unique violation.

30
00:02:27,825 --> 00:02:33,221
A state code stays stable across driver versions; a message string doesn't.

31
00:02:33,671 --> 00:02:35,506
And test type round trips.

32
00:02:35,506 --> 00:02:44,681
Postgres timestamp with time zone stores microseconds, but an Instant can carry nanoseconds, so a value can be silently truncated.

33
00:02:44,681 --> 00:02:51,527
The same kind of test catches money stored as a double and coming back with floating-point drift.

34
00:02:52,427 --> 00:02:57,120
For an outbound HTTP client, run a stub server in a container.

35
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:00,602
Here WireMock, with an explicit wait strategy.

36
00:03:00,602 --> 00:03:08,323
Stub the dispatch endpoint to return five oh three, and verify the client retried exactly three times.

37
00:03:08,323 --> 00:03:12,865
Stub it to respond after five seconds, and assert a timeout.

38
00:03:13,315 --> 00:03:20,074
Retries, timeouts and circuit breakers only exist in the interaction with a slow or failing peer.

39
00:03:20,074 --> 00:03:23,767
A Mockito mock can't say responds after five seconds.

40
00:03:23,767 --> 00:03:33,453
And never call a real third-party service from a test: it's slow, rate-limited, sometimes billed, and your build goes red when they deploy.

41
00:03:34,353 --> 00:03:36,118
Isolation is a trade-off.

42
00:03:36,118 --> 00:03:39,928
A container per method is perfectly isolated and slow.

43
00:03:39,928 --> 00:03:52,419
Rolling back a transaction is fastest, but constraint violations and deferred checks surface at commit, which never happens, so the test passes on code that fails in production.

44
00:03:52,419 --> 00:03:55,665
Truncating before each test is the sweet spot.

45
00:03:56,115 --> 00:04:02,005
To keep it fast: use static containers, or one singleton container for the whole JVM.

46
00:04:02,005 --> 00:04:11,151
Set an explicit wait strategy, because started isn't ready, and the default check is the classic works-locally, times-out-in-CI bug.

47
00:04:11,151 --> 00:04:15,516
Run integration tests in Failsafe, so the fast loop stays fast.

48
00:04:15,516 --> 00:04:19,049
And for asynchronous effects, poll with Awaitility.

49
00:04:19,049 --> 00:04:19,881
Never sleep.

50
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:21,621
Why not H2?

51
00:04:21,621 --> 00:04:23,301
It's an approximation.

52
00:04:23,301 --> 00:04:34,605
Its dialect is a compatibility mode, JSON and arrays are missing or emulated, and its error codes, locking and isolation all differ from production.

53
00:04:35,055 --> 00:04:41,176
The two seconds of startup you save buy a suite that validates a database you don't run.

54
00:04:41,176 --> 00:04:46,672
Match the production database and version, and pin the image tag, never latest.

55
00:04:46,672 --> 00:04:50,846
Moving a suite off H2 will surface real bugs it was masking.

56
00:04:50,846 --> 00:04:55,855
Do it one repository at a time, starting with the most hand-written SQL.

57
00:04:56,755 --> 00:04:58,057
Production rules.

58
00:04:58,057 --> 00:05:01,581
Match the production database and its version.

59
00:05:01,581 --> 00:05:02,730
Pin image tags.

60
00:05:02,730 --> 00:05:04,568
Run the real migrations.

61
00:05:04,568 --> 00:05:07,249
Use static or singleton containers.

62
00:05:07,249 --> 00:05:09,241
Assert on SQL state codes.

63
00:05:09,241 --> 00:05:13,224
And truncate between tests rather than rolling back.

64
00:05:13,674 --> 00:05:15,700
Never use H2 as a stand-in.

65
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:19,603
Never hard-code a container port; ask the container.

66
00:05:19,603 --> 00:05:22,380
Never call real third-party services.

67
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:28,459
Never enable container reuse in CI, where a fresh container per run is the point.

68
00:05:28,459 --> 00:05:32,511
And never let integration tests run in the inner loop.

69
00:05:33,411 --> 00:05:34,110
To recap.

70
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:41,402
Integration tests prove the assumptions unit tests can't: SQL, constraints, types and drivers.

71
00:05:41,402 --> 00:05:46,056
Run real Postgres with your real migrations on random ports.

72
00:05:46,056 --> 00:05:49,857
Test constraints and type round trips explicitly.

73
00:05:49,857 --> 00:05:54,745
And stub HTTP peers in containers to test retries and timeouts.

74
00:05:55,195 --> 00:05:59,841
Next is chapter forty-four, the last chapter: testing concurrent code.

75
00:05:59,841 --> 00:06:01,168
Thanks for watching.
